      ],
      "source": "https://www.digginsmo.gov/about-us"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s name honors a railroad official surnamed Diggins, reflecting the rail’s role in Missouri’s late‑19th‑century growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.digginsmo.gov/about-us"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before becoming Diggins, the settlement was known as Cut Throat, Stella, Centerpoint, and Linington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diggins%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Diggins sits on the southeastern edge of the Springfield Plateau, part of the Ozark Mountains in south‑central Missouri.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diggins%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village lies along U.S. Route 60, four miles east of Fordland and four miles west of Seymour.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diggins%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The James River’s headwaters are just three miles northeast of Diggins, a short drive into the surrounding hills.",
